# High-reasoning trading tasks: multi-part deliverables (assessments, incident
# reports, code reviews, due-diligence memos) — no single-number answers. Each
# expectation names the specific arithmetic, causal chain and failure modes a
# resolving answer must contain, so the rubric judge scores against substance.
{"id": "r01_rms_squareoff", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "BROKER RISK POLICY (extract): (a) intraday margin utilisation above 110% of available equity triggers an automatic RMS square-off of the position with the largest margin footprint; (b) before squaring off, the RMS desk must send one margin-call notification and allow 15 minutes for funds; (c) hedged option structures must be unwound legs-together, never one leg at a time; (d) square-offs are executed at market, and the client bears slippage.\n\nINCIDENT: A client held 2 lots of a Nifty iron condor (margin blocked: 21,000 rupees) with account equity of 25,000 rupees. At 11:04 an adverse MTM move reduced equity to 18,500 rupees. At 11:06 the RMS desk squared off ONLY the two short legs at market, leaving the two long wings open. No margin-call notification was sent. The client complains.\n\nWrite an assessment for the compliance desk: (1) was a square-off justified under the policy \u2014 show the utilisation arithmetic; (2) which specific policy clauses were violated, if any; (3) what residual risk did the desk's action create for the client; (4) what should the desk have done instead. Cite clauses by letter.", "expectation": "States utilisation was 21000/18500 = ~113.5%, above the 110% trigger, so a square-off was justified under clause (a). Identifies TWO violations: clause (b) \u2014 no margin-call notification / 15-minute window \u2014 and clause (c) \u2014 legs were unwound one side at a time. Explains the residual risk: with the short legs bought back, the remaining long wings are a debit position whose value decays, and crucially the client's margin benefit from hedging is gone... the account is left with a worthless-decaying long-only structure (and, before the buy-back completes, effectively naked exposure). Recommends notifying, waiting 15 minutes, then unwinding all four legs together. A response that says the square-off itself was unjustified fails; a response that misses either violated clause fails."}
{"id": "r02_fill_diagnosis", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "An algo desk reports: a bracket order on a Nifty option (buy 1 lot at limit 150, target 190, stop-loss trigger 130 / limit 128) filled the entry at 150 at 10:15. At 10:22 the exchange feed shows traded prices touching 128 twice within one second (10:22:03.4 and 10:22:03.9), then an immediate bounce to 141. The stop-loss did NOT fire, and the position later exited at target 190. The desk wants to know whether the order system is broken.\n\nExplain: (1) the mechanics of a stop-loss LIMIT order (trigger 130, limit 128) in a fast market \u2014 under what exact sequence it fails to execute; (2) at least two distinct explanations consistent with the feed showing 128 prints but no stop execution (consider trigger sequencing, queue position / liquidity at the limit price, and last-traded-price vs bid-ask semantics); (3) whether the outcome indicates a malfunction; (4) one concrete change to make the protection more reliable and its cost/trade-off.", "expectation": "Explains that an SL-L order becomes a LIMIT order at 128 once 130 trades/triggers: in a fast drop through 130 to 128, the order book can gap or the queue at 128 can be exhausted before this order's turn, so prints AT 128 do not guarantee a fill for a late-queued limit order; alternatively the trigger fired and the limit rested unfilled as price bounced to 141 (or trigger semantics were on LTP that skipped 130->128 between snapshots). Concludes this is NOT necessarily a malfunction \u2014 it is the designed failure mode of SL-L. Recommends SL-Market (guaranteed execution, unbounded slippage) or a wider trigger-limit gap, naming the slippage trade-off. An answer claiming the system must be broken, or one that does not distinguish trigger from execution, fails."}
{"id": "r03_code_lookahead", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Review this Python backtest for a 5-minute Nifty strategy. It reports a suspiciously high win rate. Identify every bug that inflates the backtest (there are at least two distinct ones), explain the mechanism by which each inflates results, and rewrite the loop correctly.\n\n```python\ndef backtest(candles, capital=100000):\n    # candles: list of dicts with open/high/low/close, chronological\n    pnl = 0\n    for i in range(len(candles) - 1):\n        c = candles[i]\n        nxt = candles[i + 1]\n        signal = c['close'] > c['open'] and nxt['close'] > nxt['open']\n        if signal:\n            entry = c['close']          # enter on signal candle close\n            exit_ = nxt['close']        # exit next candle\n            pnl += (exit_ - entry) * 75\n    return pnl\n```", "expectation": "Finds the look-ahead bias: the signal condition reads nxt['close'] \u2014 the NEXT candle's close \u2014 which is unknowable at entry time (the entry at c['close'] is decided using information from the future candle it then trades through; the condition nxt close > nxt open all but guarantees the exit is profitable). Also flags at least one more inflation: no transaction costs/slippage, and/or overlapping trades (consecutive signal candles reuse the same next candle, compounding P&L without capital constraint or position tracking). The corrected loop must decide the signal ONLY from candle i (e.g. two prior bullish closes), enter at c[i] close or c[i+1] open, and exit later. A review that only mentions costs but misses the look-ahead fails."}
{"id": "r04_sizing_critique", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A retail trader writes this plan: 'Capital 40,000 rupees. I will trade one lot of Nifty futures (lot 75) intraday with a 20-point stop-loss, risking 2% of capital per trade as the books recommend. Twenty points is a tight stop so my risk is small.'\n\nCritique the plan quantitatively: (1) compute the rupee risk implied by the stop and compare it to the stated 2% rule; (2) identify the contradiction; (3) compute what stop distance the 2% rule would actually allow on one futures lot, and say whether that is tradeable in practice for intraday Nifty; (4) propose two structurally different ways to make the plan self-consistent (not just 'add capital'), with one drawback of each.", "expectation": "Computes 20 points x 75 = 1,500 rupees per trade against the allowed 2% x 40,000 = 800 rupees \u2014 nearly double the stated risk cap: the plan violates its own rule. The compliant stop would be 800/75 \u2248 10.7 points, which is impractically tight for intraday Nifty noise (typical 5-minute ranges exceed it, so it would be stopped out constantly). Proposes two structurally different fixes, e.g. (a) trade defined-risk option spreads whose max loss is under 800 (drawback: capped upside / premium decay), (b) reduce effective exposure via a far smaller instrument or micro-sizing/waiting for higher capital (drawback: lower absolute returns or fewer opportunities). Merely saying 'risk less' without the 1500-vs-800 arithmetic fails."}
{"id": "r05_expiry_pin", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "It is 13:30 on monthly expiry day. A trader holds a short Nifty 24,000 straddle (sold CE at 95 and PE at 88 this morning). Spot has oscillated between 23,960 and 24,040 all day and now sits at 24,010. The combined straddle now quotes at 82. The trader asks: 'I am in profit \u2014 should I hold to expiry to collect the remaining 82, or close now?'\n\nWrite the decision memo: (1) what does holding to 15:30 actually collect if spot settles at 24,010, and what is the maximum the position can still lose if spot breaks 150 points either way in the final two hours; (2) explain gamma risk near an at-the-money strike in the final hours of expiry \u2014 why does the same 150-point move hurt far more at 14:30 than it did at 10:00; (3) name the phenomenon where spot stays near a heavy-OI strike into expiry, and say what invalidates that expectation; (4) give a concrete recommendation with a condition attached (an if-then rule, not just 'it depends').", "expectation": "Holding to settlement at 24,010 collects 82 minus the 10-point intrinsic (settle 24,010 means the CE settles 10 ITM), i.e. about 72 more points; a 150-point break costs roughly 150-82 \u2248 68+ points beyond what is already banked (the straddle's remaining premium is dwarfed by intrinsic explosion). Explains expiry-day gamma: near-ATM short options approach a step function at expiry \u2014 delta flips violently with small spot moves and there is no time premium left to buffer, so late moves translate one-for-one into loss. Names pinning (max-pain / pin risk at heavy-OI strikes) and notes a news shock or large directional flow invalidates it. Gives an if-then rule, e.g. hold while spot stays inside roughly 23,950-24,050 but exit immediately on a close beyond it, or take profit given risk/reward has collapsed. A memo without the intrinsic-vs-premium arithmetic or without a conditional recommendation fails."}
{"id": "r06_stt_trap", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A trader bought one lot (75) of the Nifty 24,000 CE at 70 rupees per unit. On expiry Nifty settles at 24,080, so the option is 80 points in the money. The trader plans to let it expire and be exercised, reasoning: 'intrinsic 80 > premium 70, so I lock a profit; selling it now at the 78 bid only gives me 8 points.'\n\nAudit that reasoning: (1) compute the P&L if the option is SOLD at 78 before close, net of a flat 25-rupee brokerage-and-charges estimate; (2) compute the P&L if the option is allowed to EXERCISE, knowing that exercised/assigned long index options attract STT at 0.125% of the full settlement value (settlement price x lot), unlike the 0.1% on sell-premium for a normal sale \u2014 show the STT amount; (3) explain WHY the exchange levies STT on the settlement notional for exercised options and what behaviour this rule is designed to discourage; (4) state the general rule the trader should adopt for near-the-money longs on expiry day.", "expectation": "Selling at 78: (78-70) x 75 = 600 minus ~25 charges \u2248 +575. Exercising: intrinsic (80-70) x 75 = 750 gross, but STT = 0.125% x 24,080 x 75 \u2248 2,258, producing a NET LOSS of roughly -1,508 despite finishing in the money. Explains the levy is charged on settlement notional to make cash-settled exercise bear tax comparable to delivery/at-notional trading and to discourage leaving marginally-ITM options to auto-exercise (post-2019 the trap is narrower but the notional-based STT on exercise remains the point). General rule: SELL near-the-money long options before expiry close rather than letting them auto-exercise unless intrinsic comfortably exceeds the settlement-notional STT. An answer that treats intrinsic-minus-premium as the exercise P&L without the STT computation fails."}
{"id": "r07_hedge_review", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A portfolio holds: long 2 lots Nifty futures (entry 24,000), and the manager 'hedged' by selling 2 lots of the 24,200 CE at 60. The manager claims: 'I am hedged \u2014 if the market falls, the call premium cushions me.'\n\nAssess the hedge: (1) compute the portfolio P&L per unit at expiry spots of 23,600, 24,000, 24,260 and 24,600 \u2014 show the working; (2) state what fraction of a 400-point fall the 60-point premium actually cushions; (3) explain why this structure (covered call) is NOT a hedge against the risk the manager described, and name the exposure profile it actually creates; (4) propose the structure that would express the manager's stated goal (downside protection) and quantify its cost using a 24,000-strike put quoted at 85.", "expectation": "P&L per unit (futures P&L plus premium minus call intrinsic): at 23,600 -> -400 + 60 = -340; at 24,000 -> 0 + 60 = +60; at 24,260 -> +260 + 60 - 60 = +260; at 24,600 -> +600 + 60 - 400 = +260 \u2014 gains cap at +260 for any spot above 24,200. The 60-point premium cushions only 15% of a 400-point fall. Correctly names the structure a covered call: full downside retained minus a small credit, upside capped \u2014 the opposite of the downside protection the manager claimed. The stated goal needs a protective put: buying the 24,000 PE at 85 caps downside at 85 per unit below entry, at a cost of 85 x 75 x 2 = 12,750 for the 2-lot position. Numbers matter: an answer without the four-spot P&L working or the ~15% cushioning figure fails."}
{"id": "r08_perf_claims", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A fund's marketing one-pager claims: 'Our intraday options strategy returned 15% in its first 45 days. Annualised, that is over 200%. The strategy has an 85% win rate across 40 trades, proving a durable edge. Maximum drawdown was only 4%, so risk is minimal.'\n\nWrite the due-diligence review: (1) verify the annualisation arithmetic and then explain why compounding a 45-day return to a year is misleading for an intraday strategy (name at least two distinct reasons \u2014 think capacity/market-regime and the difference between arithmetic extrapolation and achievable compounding); (2) evaluate whether 40 trades at 85% win rate 'proves' an edge \u2014 quantify roughly how likely such a streak is for a strategy whose true win rate is only 60% (an approximate binomial argument is fine) and state what additional statistic the win rate omits; (3) explain why a 4% max drawdown over 45 days does not imply 'minimal risk' for an options-selling strategy specifically; (4) list the three documents/records you would demand before allocating.", "expectation": "Confirms (1.15)^(365/45) - 1 \u2248 210%, then rejects the extrapolation: 45 days is one market regime (no drawdown periods sampled), intraday strategies have capacity limits so returns do not scale with compounded capital, and past short-window compounding assumes redeployment at the same edge. For the win rate: P(>=34 wins in 40 | p=0.6) is on the order of a few in ten-thousand \u2014 so 85%/40 is suggestive, BUT win rate omits the payoff ratio: an options seller can win 85% of small credits and lose it all on the 15% (negative skew), so expectancy, not win rate, is the test. The 4% drawdown point must connect to short-vol tail risk: the loss distribution is fat-tailed and 45 days without a vol event proves little. Demands records like broker-verified P&L/contract notes, full trade log with timestamps, and fee/slippage-inclusive returns. An answer that accepts the annualisation or treats win rate as sufficient fails."}
{"id": "r09_incident_triage", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Production incident from a retail algo-trading stack, 09:15-09:32 IST. Facts: (a) the strategy places bracket orders via broker API; (b) from 09:15:04, every order fails with 'RMS:Rule: Check circuit limit including square off order exceeds'; (c) the same account places identical orders successfully from 09:31; (d) the instrument was a far-OTM weekly option whose previous close was 2.15; (e) the strategy's limit price is computed as previous_close * 1.5 rounded to the nearest 0.05; (f) no code deployed overnight; (g) other instruments traded fine throughout.\n\nWrite the incident report: (1) root-cause the rejections \u2014 what about (d)+(e) interacts with exchange circuit/price-band rules in the opening minutes; (2) explain why it self-resolved by 09:31 without any intervention; (3) say why 'no code deployed' does not mean 'no code defect', identifying the latent defect; (4) give the corrective fix and one regression test that would have caught it.", "expectation": "Root cause: far-OTM options carry tight exchange price bands around the reference price in the opening session; previous_close x 1.5 = ~3.20 breached the band for a 2.15-priced option, so RMS rejected the order (the circuit-limit rule), while other instruments' 1.5x limits stayed inside their bands. It self-resolved because bands are recalculated/widened as the option starts trading (or the reference updated with fresh trades), so by 09:31 3.20 was inside the band. The latent defect: the limit-price formula ignores exchange price bands \u2014 a data-dependent bug that only manifests on low-priced instruments, which is why no deployment was needed to trigger it. Fix: clamp the computed limit to the instrument's current band (fetched from the API) with a fallback; regression test: an order-building unit test with a 2.15 previous close asserting the limit never exceeds the band. An answer blaming broker outage or account margin fails."}
{"id": "r10_strategy_conflict", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A desk runs two automated strategies on the same account: S1 sells a 09:20 Nifty straddle and holds to 15:00 with a 25% premium stop per leg. S2 is a 10:30 trend-follower that buys ATM options in the trend direction with a 40% premium stop. On trending days the desk notices the account loses MORE than either strategy's backtest predicts, and margin utilisation spikes around 10:30-11:15.\n\nDiagnose: (1) explain the structural conflict \u2014 what does S2's entry do to the account's net position given S1 is already short both sides, and why do trending days specifically hurt; (2) why is the COMBINED loss worse than the sum of the independent backtests (consider margin interaction and stop dynamics \u2014 S1's losing leg and S2's entry are correlated); (3) why does margin spike at exactly 10:30-11:15; (4) propose two desk-level fixes that preserve both strategies' independent logic, and state the residual risk each leaves.", "expectation": "Explains that on a trending day S1's short option on the trend side is losing while S2 then BUYS an ATM option in the same direction \u2014 partially offsetting S1's short (reducing the hedge S1's stop assumed) yet booked as a separate position: the account is simultaneously short and long similar strikes, so S1's stop triggers on its losing leg at the same time S2 pays fresh premium, realising both costs in the same move (correlated, not independent, losses \u2014 the backtests assumed independence). Margin spikes at 10:30-11:15 because S2's long entry initially adds margin while S1's short strangle margin inflates with the adverse move (peak overlap window before S1's stop clears). Fixes such as: a desk-level net-position/margin coordinator that nets or blocks conflicting entries (residual: S2 misses some trends), or running the strategies in separate accounts/sub-limits (residual: total capital fragmentation, S1's stop still fires). An answer that says 'they hedge each other so it is fine' fails."}
